<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The hardened runtime is what stops DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES loading someone
	  else's code into the app, and stops another process attaching to it. Both
	  are worth having and neither costs anything.

	  Library validation has to be given up to get them. It requires every
	  loaded library to carry the same Team ID as the process loading it, and an
	  ad-hoc signature carries no Team ID at all — so the bundled ffmpeg is
	  refused its own vendored libavcodec, and the app will not start. A Team ID
	  needs the Apple Developer Program; until there is one, this is the trade.

	  What that leaves open: anything already running as the user can replace a
	  dylib inside the bundle on disk. Nothing here prevents that. It is the same
	  exposure as replacing the executable itself, which no signature stops
	  either once Gatekeeper has been told to stand down.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
